Juti Boon Soong took over the mining business
Local databases indicate that Mr. Juti Boon Soong bought the Takua Pa Valley dredging company and discovered commuting difficulties for workers and locals between the old market and the company area.
Construction of Khok Khanoon iron bridge
Iron from a decommissioned tin dredger was used to build a bridge over 200 metres long across the Takua Pa River, connecting the town with the mining area and factory.
Featured as a location in the film Wonderful Town
Articles tracking filming locations state that the bridge was a setting in the Thai film Wonderful Town. This is not verified by official production documents, only by audience articles.
Municipality included the bridge entrance area in budget documents
Discovered municipal budget documents contain an item for the maintenance and improvement of land and structures around the entrance of the Boon Soong iron bridge. This is not evidence of a restoration of the bridge itself.
NBT reported the bridge as a mining heritage site
NBT reported that the bridge is a historical landmark and was temporarily closed that week to accommodate the filming of a foreign movie.
A wooden bridge was built for workers to walk and cycle across the canal, as well as for locals to transport goods, but the reviewed sources do not specify the exact year.
No image authorized for publication yet
Steel structure about 200 metres long
The bridge spans the Takua Pa river and connects the main road area with the old ore dredger site.
Built with steel from a decommissioned tin dredger, and the old dredger can be seen at the end of the bridge.
From the bridge, green fields, trees, and mountain ranges are visible, making it suitable for sightseeing and photography.
The bridge is still a route used by villagers, so one should be careful of vehicles while visiting.
Inviting check-ins at Boon Soong iron bridge, mining heritage of Takua Pa town
NBT presented the bridge as a mining heritage site and historical landmark, noting its temporary closure for a foreign film production.
The iron bridge structure was built using steel from a decommissioned tin dredger, not new steel produced specifically for the bridge.
Before the iron bridge, workers and villagers had to use rowboats to cross the canal, and there used to be a wooden bridge for walking and cycling.
The bridge appeared in a location tracking article for the film Wonderful Town (2007).
Travel along Rat Bamrung Road towards Takua Pa old town; the bridge is on the left, about 2 kilometres from the town centre.
Information not verified · Open to visitors all day (information from a secondary tourism source)
Reviews state that roadside parking is available with limited space, but this is not verified by official sources
Travel along Rat Bamrung Road towards Takua Pa old town; the bridge is on the left, about 2 kilometres from the town centre.
In 1968 (B.E. 2511), steel from a mining dredger was used to build the Khok Khanun Iron Bridge, more than 200 metres long, across the Takua Pa River. The initiative came from Juti Boonsung, owner of Juti Boonsung Mining Dredger Co., Ltd., founded in 1960 (B.E. 2503).
The bridge crosses the Takua Pa River, linking the main road area with the former mining plant. It uses steel from a decommissioned mining dredger, and the old plant is still visible at the far end. From the bridge, you can see green fields, trees, and mountain ranges, making it suitable for views and photography.
